# THE SUITE-SIZE RATCHET -- the single source of truth for how many tests exist.
# Created 2026-07-13 (roadmap 6.14).
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS
# -------------------
# The G1 pre-flight gate carried a hard-coded pytest floor. It rotted FIVE TIMES IN TWO DAYS:
#
#     1485  ->  1805  ->  1842  ->  1850  ->  1853
#
# Every single time, the number sat directly beneath an emphatic, all-capitals comment
# ordering the next person to raise it -- written by the person who then failed to raise it.
# At 1485 against a suite of 1,815, THREE HUNDRED AND THIRTY tests could have silently
# vanished -- deleted, mis-named, or lost to a collection error -- and the gate would still
# have reported PASS. A floor that drifts below reality is not a floor; it is a rubber stamp.
#
# THE COMMENT DOES NOT ENFORCE ITSELF, AND NO VOLUME OF EMPHASIS WILL MAKE IT.
# That is roadmap section 7, root pattern (a): a number written down once and never
# re-derived becomes a lie on a schedule. The fix is never a louder comment. The fix is to
# make FORGETTING FAIL.
#
# HOW IT WORKS
# -----------
# `tests/conftest.py` reads this file. Under the explicit `--assert-suite-size` flag it
# compares the number below against the number of tests pytest actually COLLECTED, and
# ABORTS THE RUN on any disagreement -- in either direction:
#
#     collected < expected   ->  tests have VANISHED. Something is silently not running.
#     collected > expected   ->  tests were ADDED and this file was not updated.
#
# Both are failures. The second is the ratchet: you cannot add a test without bumping this
# number, because the gate goes red until you do. Forgetting is no longer possible.
#
# G1 (`scripts/Run_Preflight_Local.ps1`) and Continuous Integration (`.github/workflows/
# ci.yml`) BOTH pass `--assert-suite-size`, and BOTH read this file. The number therefore
# lives in exactly ONE place. It previously lived in four, and disagreed with itself in
# three of them.
#
# Running a subset locally (`pytest tests/unit/test_foo.py`) is UNAFFECTED: without the
# explicit flag, the check does not run at all.
#
# WHY *COLLECTED* AND NOT *PASSED*
# --------------------------------
# The collected count is ENVIRONMENT-INDEPENDENT. The passed/skipped split is not:
#
#     2026-07-13, Windows + full data:   1863 passed +  7 skipped              = 1870
#     2026-07-13, Linux CI runner:       1856 passed + 13 skipped + 1 xfailed  = 1870
#
# Same collection, different outcomes -- Continuous Integration legitimately skips the
# Windows-targeted post-flight tests, the ESM-2 tests (HuggingFace Hub is offline by design),
# and the tests needing gitignored cohort data. Asserting `passed` would force two numbers
# and re-create the divergence this file exists to kill. Asserting `collected` gives ONE
# number that is true everywhere.
#
# NOTE: this only works because the environments now COLLECT the same tests. Before
# 2026-07-13 they did not -- `torch_geometric`, `pandera`, `pyspark`, `river` and `mapie`
# were missing from Continuous Integration, and a module-level `importorskip` collapses N
# tests into ONE skip entry, so CI collected 1825 against a local 1868. Closing that gap
# (roadmap 6.17) is what made this ratchet possible at all.
#
# WHEN YOU ADD OR REMOVE TESTS
# ----------------------------
# Run the full suite, read the collected count (passed + skipped + xfailed + xpassed), and
# update the number below IN THE SAME COMMIT. The suite will tell you the exact figure and
# refuse to go green until you do.
#
# Format: exactly one line below that is a bare positive integer. Comments and blank lines
# are ignored. Anything else is a hard error -- a malformed ratchet is a dead ratchet.
#
# HISTORY -- every change to this number, with the reason. Keep it.
#
#   1870  2026-07-13  first value. Windows (Python 3.12): 1863 passed + 7 skipped.
#                     Linux runner (3.11 and 3.12): 1856 passed + 13 skipped + 1 xfailed.
#                     Same collection, different outcomes -- hence `collected`, not `passed`.
#
#   1882  2026-07-13  +12: tests/unit/test_suite_size_ratchet.py -- the ratchet's own
#                     self-test (roadmap 6.9: a guard with no self-test is a rumour).
#
#                     THE RATCHET'S FIRST ACT WAS TO CATCH ITS OWN AUTHOR. Adding those 12
#                     tests without touching this number turned the suite RED immediately:
#
#                         SUITE-SIZE RATCHET FAILED (roadmap 6.14)
#                           expected (tests/EXPECTED_SUITE_SIZE): 1870
#                           actually collected:                   1882
#                           12 MORE test(s) than expected.
#                           Set the value to 1882 IN THE SAME COMMIT as the tests.
#
#                     pytest exit code 4. That is the whole design working: five times in two
#                     days a hard-coded floor went stale beneath an all-capitals comment
#                     ordering someone to raise it, and five times the comment did nothing.
#                     This cannot be walked past.
1882
